2.3 Database Layer (database.py)
from sqlalchemy import create_engine
from sqlalchemy.ext.declarative import declarative_base
from sqlalchemy.orm import sessionmaker
Three Core Concepts:

1. Engine - The Database Connection
engine = create_engine(
    settings.database_url,
    echo=True if settings.ENVIRONMENT == "development" else False
)
What: Database connection pool manager
Why: Reuses connections instead of creating new ones (expensive)
How:

Application starts:
    ↓
create_engine() creates connection pool:
    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
    │  Connection Pool (20 conns) │
    │  [C1][C2][C3]...[C20]      │
    └─────────────────────────────┘
    ↓
Request 1 arrives:
    - Borrows C1 from pool
    - Executes query
    - Returns C1 to pool
    ↓
Request 2 arrives:
    - Borrows C2 from pool
    - Executes query
    - Returns C2 to pool

Without Pool:
    - Each request creates new connection (slow!)
    - Database exhausts available connections
    - Crashes under load
echo=True Effect:

# Development mode (echo=True):
>>> db.query(Project).all()
SQL: SELECT * FROM dbo.tpsm_projects  # Printed to console
[<Project id=1>, <Project id=2>]

# Production mode (echo=False):
>>> db.query(Project).all()
[<Project id=1>, <Project id=2>]  # No SQL output
2. SessionLocal - Transaction Manager
SessionLocal = sessionmaker(autocommit=False, autoflush=False, bind=engine)
What: Factory that creates database sessions
Why: Each request needs isolated transaction
How:

# autocommit=False means:
db.add(project)      # Data in memory only
db.add(response)     # Still in memory
db.commit()          # NOW write to database (atomic)

# If autocommit=True (bad!):
db.add(project)      # Immediately writes to DB
db.add(response)     # Immediately writes to DB
# What if response fails? Project already saved! Inconsistent data!

# autoflush=False means:
db.add(project)           # Queued in memory
project_id = project.id   # ERROR! Not in DB yet
db.flush()                # Now writes to DB temporarily
project_id = project.id   # Works! ID assigned
db.commit()               # Makes it permanent
3. Base - Model Definition Parent
Base = declarative_base()
What: Parent class for all database models
Why: Tracks model definitions for schema creation
How:

# When you define:
class Project(Base):
    __tablename__ = "tpsm_projects"
    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)

# Behind the scenes:
Base.metadata.tables["tpsm_projects"] = <Table definition>

# When create_all() is called:
for table_name, table_def in Base.metadata.tables.items():
    execute_sql(table_def.create_statement())
Dependency Injection - get_db()
def get_db():
    db = SessionLocal()
    try:
        yield db
    finally:
        db.close()
What: Context manager for database sessions
Why: Ensures session is always closed (prevent memory leaks)
How:

# In route:
@router.get("/projects")
def get_projects(db: Session = Depends(get_db)):
    # FastAPI magic:
    # 1. Calls get_db()
    # 2. Creates session
    # 3. Passes to db parameter
    projects = db.query(Project).all()
    return projects
    # 4. After return, FastAPI calls finally block
    # 5. Session closed automatically

# Without Depends():
@router.get("/projects")
def get_projects():
    db = SessionLocal()
    projects = db.query(Project).all()
    # Oops! Forgot to close()
    # Session leaks, eventually runs out of connections
Lifecycle Diagram:

Request arrives
    ↓
FastAPI calls get_db()
    ↓
SessionLocal() creates new session
    ↓
yield db (session passed to route function)
    ↓
Route executes (queries, commits, etc.)
    ↓
Route returns response
    ↓
finally block executes
    ↓
db.close() releases connection back to pool
    ↓
Response sent to client
2.4 Models Layer (models.py)
Project Model - The Core Entity
class Project(Base):
    __tablename__ = "tpsm_projects"
    __table_args__ = {'schema': 'dbo'}
    
    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True, index=True)
    project_name = Column(String(255), nullable=False)
    environment = Column(String(50), nullable=False)
    created_at = Column(DateTime(timezone=True), server_default=func.now())
    
    # Relationships
    responses = relationship("Response", back_populates="project", cascade="all, delete-orphan")
Line-by-Line Explanation:

__tablename__ = "tpsm_projects"

Maps Python class to database table name
SQLAlchemy will look for table named "tpsm_projects"
__table_args__ = {'schema': 'dbo'}

Sets database schema (namespace for tables)
Full table name becomes: dbo.tpsm_projects
In SQL Server: schemas organize tables (like folders for files)
id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True, index=True)

Column: Declares a table column
Integer: Data type (maps to SQL INT)
primary_key=True: Unique identifier for each row
Auto-incrementing (IDENTITY in SQL Server)
Never null
Indexed automatically
index=True: Creates separate index for faster lookups
project_name = Column(String(255), nullable=False)

String(255): Maps to NVARCHAR(255) in SQL Server
nullable=False: SQL NOT NULL constraint
Database rejects INSERT/UPDATE without this field
created_at = Column(DateTime(timezone=True), server_default=func.now())

DateTime(timezone=True): Stores timestamp with timezone info
Maps to DATETIME2 in SQL Server
Prevents timezone confusion
server_default=func.now(): Database sets timestamp automatically
Uses database's GETDATE() function
Even if Python doesn't provide value, database fills it in
Relationships - The Magic of ORM

responses = relationship("Response", back_populates="project", cascade="all, delete-orphan")
What: Creates Python object navigation
Why: Access related data without writing SQL
How:

# Without relationship:
project = db.query(Project).filter(Project.id == 1).first()
responses = db.query(Response).filter(Response.project_id == project.id).all()

# With relationship:
project = db.query(Project).filter(Project.id == 1).first()
responses = project.responses  # Automatic SQL JOIN!

# SQLAlchemy generates:
# SELECT * FROM tpsm_responses WHERE project_id = 1
Cascade Options:

cascade="all, delete-orphan"
What Happens:

# Scenario 1: Delete project
project = db.query(Project).get(1)
db.delete(project)
db.commit()
# Automatically deletes ALL responses for this project!
# No orphaned responses left behind

# Scenario 2: Remove response from project
project.responses.remove(some_response)
db.commit()
# Response is deleted from database (orphan removal)

# Without cascade:
db.delete(project)
# ERROR! Foreign key constraint violation
# Must manually delete all responses first
back_populates - Bidirectional Relationship:

# In Project model:
responses = relationship("Response", back_populates="project")

# In Response model:
project = relationship("Project", back_populates="responses")

# Usage:
project = db.query(Project).get(1)
response = project.responses[0]
response.project  # Goes back to parent project!
FileMetadata Model - Large-Scale Data
class FileMetadata(Base):
    __tablename__ = "tpsm_file_metadata"
    
    id = Column(BigInteger, primary_key=True, index=True)
    size_bytes = Column(BigInteger, nullable=True)
