3.3 Index Strategy
-- Primary Key Index (automatic):
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX PK_projects ON tpsm_projects(id);

-- Foreign Key Index (manual):
CREATE INDEX IX_responses_project ON tpsm_responses(project_id);

-- Composite Index (query optimization):
CREATE INDEX IX_metadata_project_date 
ON tpsm_file_metadata(project_id, modified_date);
Why Indexes Matter:

-- Without index:
SELECT * FROM tpsm_file_metadata WHERE project_id = 1;
-- Scans ALL 1,000,000 rows (Full Table Scan)
-- Time: 30 seconds

-- With index:
-- Uses B-Tree index to jump directly to matching rows
-- Reads only 10,000 rows for project_id = 1
-- Time: 0.5 seconds
Index Trade-off:

Pros:
+ Faster SELECT queries (10-1000x speedup)
+ Faster WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY

Cons:
- Slower INSERT (must update index)
- Slower UPDATE (must update index if indexed column changes)
- More storage space (index stored separately)

Rule of Thumb:
- Index columns in WHERE clauses
- Index foreign keys
- Index columns in JOIN conditions
- Don't index low-cardinality columns (e.g., boolean)
