    Result: FALSE

Check Archive tier:
    Modified 180-1095 days ago? NO (1116 > 1095)
    Result: FALSE

Check Delete candidates:
    Modified > 3 years ago? YES (1116 > 1095)
    Accessed > 2 years ago? YES (1116 > 730)
    Matches temp pattern? Check filename...
        Contains "backup"? YES
    Result: DELETE CANDIDATE ✅
Safety Logic for Delete Tier:

# Multiple conditions required (AND logic):
delete_candidates = db.query(...).filter(
    FileMetadata.modified_date < now - timedelta(days=1095),  # > 3 years
    or_(
        FileMetadata.accessed_date < now - timedelta(days=730),  # > 2 years
        FileMetadata.accessed_date.is_(None)
    ),
    or_(  # Filename patterns
        func.lower(file_name).like('%temp%'),
        func.lower(file_name).like('%backup%'),
        func.lower(file_name).like('%old%'),
        func.lower(directory_path).like('%temp%'),
        func.lower(directory_path).like('%cache%')
    )
)
Why Conservative Approach?

Aggressive (dangerous):
    Delete anything > 1 year old
    Risk: Delete important archival data

Conservative (safe):
    Delete only if:
    - Very old (> 3 years)
    - Not accessed recently (> 2 years)
    - Matches suspicious patterns (temp, backup, cache)
    Risk: Minimal false positives
    
Real-world example:
    File: "CompanyContract_2020.pdf"
    - Modified: 4 years ago
    - Accessed: 3 years ago
    - Pattern: None
    Result: NOT deleted (legal/compliance risk!)
    
    File: "temp_backup_2020.tmp"
    - Modified: 4 years ago
    - Accessed: 3 years ago
    - Pattern: temp + backup
    Result: DELETE candidate ✅
